## 7. TL;DR – What you need to change in your code

```js
// 1️⃣ Imports
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

// 2️⃣ Namespace
//   mapboxgl → maplibregl everywhere

// 3️⃣ No access token
//   delete mapboxgl.accessToken;

// 4️⃣ Style URL
style: 'https://my

Below is a quick-reference guide to every practical way you can add a **search-box (geocoder) UI** to a map that is built with **Mapbox GL JS v2**.  
It’s split into three sections:

| # | What it is | How you hook it to Mapbox GL JS v2 | Token / cost | When you’d pick it |
|---|------------|-----------------------------------|--------------|--------------------|
| **A** | **Mapbox-provided Geocoder** (official plugin) | `new MapboxGeocoder({ accessToken: MAPBOX_TOKEN, mapboxgl: mapboxgl })` | Uses your Mapbox access token → counts against your Mapbox usage (free tier = 50 k requests/mo, then pay-as-you-go) | You already have a Mapbox token, need the fastest “drop-in” solution, and are fine with Mapbox’s terms. |
| **B** | **MapLibre-GL-Geocoder** (community fork of the Mapbox plugin) | Same API as the Mapbox plugin, but you import from `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` and pass a *different* geocoding service URL (or keep the Mapbox one) | No extra token if you point it at a free service (Nominatim, Photon, etc.). If you keep the Mapbox endpoint you still need a token. | You want the same UI as Mapbox’s plugin but want to use an open geocoder or you are already migrating to MapLibre. |
| **C** | **Self-hosted / third-party geocoder services** (Nominatim, Photon, Pelias, OpenCage, MapTiler, HERE, Google Places, Algolia Places, etc.) | Build your own UI (HTML + JS) or reuse an existing UI component (e.g. `@mapbox/search-box`, `leaflet-geosearch`, `autocomplete.js`). When the user selects a result, call `map.flyTo({center: [lng, lat], zoom: …})`. | Most are free for low volume (Nominatim public instance, Photon demo) but have usage policies; paid tiers exist for higher volume or SLA. | You need full control over data source, want to avoid Mapbox fees, or must comply with a specific licensing / attribution requirement. |
| **D** | **Hybrid / “wrapper” libraries** (e.g. `mapbox-gl-geocoder` with a custom `geocoder` option) | Pass a custom `geocoder` object that implements the same `forwardGeocode` / `reverseGeocode` methods the Mapbox plugin expects. | Same as the underlying service you choose. | You like the Mapbox-Geocoder UI but want to swap the backend without rewriting UI code. |
| **E** | **Full-featured UI frameworks** (React-Map-GL, Vue-Mapbox-GL, Angular wrappers) | Use the component that ships with the framework (`<Geocoder />`, `<MapboxGeocoder />`, etc.) – they internally use the Mapbox-Geocoder plugin or a custom one. | Same as the underlying service. | You’re already using a component-based UI library and want a declarative approach. |

Below you’ll find **detailed code snippets**, **pros/cons**, and a **decision-tree** to help you pick the right option for your project.

---

## 1️⃣ Option A – The official Mapbox Geocoder plugin

### When to use it
* You already have a Mapbox access token and are happy to pay for Mapbox’s geocoding API (or stay within the free tier).  
* You want the *exact* UI that appears on Mapbox’s own examples (autocomplete dropdown, “search for places” placeholder, “use current location” button).  
* You need the quickest possible integration – just a few lines of code.

### Install

```bash
npm i @mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder
# or, if you prefer a CDN:
# <script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v4.7.2/mapbox-gl-geocoder.min.js"></script>
# <link href="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v4.7.2/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css" rel="stylesheet" />
```

### Basic usage (ES-modules)

```js
import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';
import MapboxGeocoder from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder';
import '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder/dist/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css';

mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_TOKEN';

const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
});

const geocoder = new MapboxGeocoder({
  accessToken: mapboxgl.accessToken,
  mapboxgl: mapboxgl,          // required for the built-in UI
  placeholder: 'Search places…',
  marker: false                // we’ll add our own marker later if we want
});

map.addControl(geocoder, 'top-left');   // or 'top-right', 'bottom-left', …
```

### What you get “for free”

| Feature | Details |
|---------|---------|
| **Autocomplete** | As the user types, the plugin calls `https://api.mapbox.com/geocoding/v5/mapbox.places/<query>.json` and shows the top 5-10 results. |
| **Proximity bias** | If you set `proximity: [lng, lat]` the results are weighted toward the map’s current center. |
| **Bounding-box bias** | `bbox: [west, south, east, north]` restricts results to a region. |
| **Reverse geocode on click** | `geocoder.on('result', e => …)` gives you the full GeoJSON feature (`e.result`). |
| **Built-in “use my location” button** | `geocoder.setRenderFunction` can hide it; otherwise it appears automatically. |
| **Internationalization** | Pass `language: 'fr'` or `countries: ['us','ca']`. |
| **Custom result rendering** | Override `geocoder.render` to change the dropdown UI. |

### Pros & Cons

| Pros | Cons |
|------|------|
| One-line integration, fully maintained by Mapbox. | Tied to Mapbox’s proprietary geocoding API → you must keep a valid token and pay for usage beyond the free tier. |
| Handles rate-limiting, caching, and error handling internally. | If you ever want to move off Mapbox (e.g., to MapLibre) you’ll have to replace the plugin. |
| Works with Mapbox-GL-JS v2 out-of-the-box. | The UI is styled for Mapbox’s default CSS; customizing it beyond the provided hooks can be fiddly. |

---

## 2️⃣ Option B – MapLibre-GL-Geocoder (open-source fork)

If you **plan to migrate to MapLibre** (or you already run a MapLibre map) you can keep the exact same UI while swapping the backend to any geocoder you like.

### Install

```bash
npm i @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder
# CDN (v4.7.2 at time of writing)
# <script src="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder@4.7.2/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.min.js"></script>
# <link href="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder@4.7.2/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.css" rel="stylesheet" />
```

### Using the Mapbox backend (still works)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';
import '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.css';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty',
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
});

const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  accessToken: 'YOUR_MAPBOX_TOKEN',   // still needed if you point at Mapbox
  maplibregl: maplibregl,
  placeholder: 'Search…'
});

map.addControl(geocoder);
```

### Swapping to a **free** service (e.g., Nominatim)

```js
const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  // No accessToken needed for Nominatim
  maplibregl: maplibregl,
  placeholder: 'Search…',
  // The `geocoder` option lets you provide a custom backend that follows the same API
  geocoder: new (class {
    forwardGeocode(config) {
      const url = new URL('https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search');
      url.search = new URLSearchParams({
        q: config.query,
        format: 'geojson',
        addressdetails: 1,
        limit: 5,
        // optional: viewbox, bounded, countrycodes, etc.
      });
      return fetch(url)
        .then(res => res.json())
        .then(data => ({
          // MapLibre-Geocoder expects an object with a `features` array
          features: data.features.map(f => ({
            type: 'Feature',
            geometry: f.geometry,
            properties: {
              // MapLibre-Geocoder uses `place_name` for the dropdown label
              place_name: f.properties.display_name,
              // any other properties you want to expose
            }
          }))
        }));
    }
    reverseGeocode(config) {
      // optional – implement if you need reverse lookup
      return Promise.resolve({features: []});
    }
  })()
});
```

### Pros & Cons

| Pros | Cons |
|------|------|
| Same UI as Mapbox’s official plugin – no redesign needed. | You must write (or copy) a thin wrapper for the geocoder you choose. |
| Works with **any** HTTP-based geocoder that returns GeoJSON (Nominatim, Photon, Pelias, MapTiler, etc.). | Some services (e.g., Nominatim) have strict usage policies; you may need to self-host for production. |
| Fully open-source (BSD-3) – no hidden licensing. | The plugin is community-maintained; occasional lag behind Mapbox’s UI updates. |
| Works with both Mapbox-GL-JS v2 **and** MapLibre-GL-JS (v6+). | If you stay on Mapbox-GL-JS v2 you still need a Mapbox token for the Mapbox backend. |

---

## 3️⃣ Option C – Build your own UI + any geocoder service

If you want **complete control** over the look-and-feel, or you already have a UI library you love (React-Autocomplete, Material-UI Autocomplete, Vue-Select, etc.), you can skip the bundled plugin entirely.

### Typical flow

1. **User types → UI component fires a request** to a geocoder endpoint (e.g., `https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?...`).  
2. **Parse the response** (usually GeoJSON or a custom JSON format).  
3. **Show a dropdown** of suggestions (your UI library handles this).  
4. **When a suggestion is chosen**, call `map.flyTo({center: [lng, lat], zoom: 14})` (or `map.easeTo`).  
5. (Optional) **Add a marker** at the result location.

### Minimal vanilla-JS example (Nominatim)

```html
<input id="search" placeholder="Search…" style="position:absolute;top:10px;left:10px;z-index:1;padding:6px;">
<div id="suggestions" style="position:absolute;top:40px;left:10px;z-index:1;background:#fff;border:1px solid #ccc;"></div>
<div id="map" style="width:100%;height:100vh;"></div>

<script type="module">
import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

const map = new maplibreg
